'''Shinji Kagawa''' ({{langx|ja|香川 真司}}, ''Kagawa Shinji'' {{IPA|ja|kaꜜɡawa ɕiꜜndʑi|}}; born 17 March 1989) is a Japanese professional [[Association football|footballer]] who plays as a [[midfielder]] for [[J1 League]] club [[Cerezo Osaka]]. He is widely regarded as one of the best Japanese players of all time.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.sportsmole.co.uk/football/japan/world-cup-top-10/feature/top-10-japanese-footballers-of-all-time_154944.html|title=FIFA World Cup countdown: Top 10 Japanese footballers of all time|website=Sportsmole.com|date=15 May 2014 |access-date=21 February 2022|archive-date=21 February 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220221223731/https://www.sportsmole.co.uk/football/japan/world-cup-top-10/feature/top-10-japanese-footballers-of-all-time_154944.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.90min.com/posts/japan-s-greatest-xi-2000-2020-01e70fxqa8ma|title=Japan's Greatest XI – 2000 – 2020|date=28 April 2020|access-date=21 February 2022|website=[[90min.com]]|archive-date=21 February 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220221223724/https://www.90min.com/posts/japan-s-greatest-xi-2000-2020-01e70fxqa8ma|url-status=live}}</ref>

Kagawa began his professional career in Japan with Cerezo Osaka, where he became the first midfielder in J.League history to claim the top scorer title, playing as a shadow striker. In the 2009 season, he formed a partnership with [[Takashi Inui]], which played a role in his emergence as the J2 League's top scorer. He led the J2 League in both goals and assists that season.<ref name="セレッソ大阪25周年記念サイト">{{Cite web|url=https://www.cerezo.jp/special/25th_anniversary/#history16 |title=セレッソ大阪25周年記念サイト |publisher =セレッソ大阪オフィシャルサイト |accessdate=2021-04-26}}</ref>

He joined [[Borussia Dortmund]] in 2010. After two years with Dortmund, Kagawa signed for [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] on a four-year contract. Two years later, he returned to Dortmund.<ref name="ShinjisReturn"/> He later played for [[Beşiktaş J.K.|Beşiktaş]] in Turkey, [[Real Zaragoza]] in Spain, [[PAOK FC|PAOK]] in Greece, and [[Sint-Truidense V.V.|Sint-Truiden]] in Belgium before returning to Japan, rejoining Cerezo Osaka at the end of January 2023. Kagawa holds the record for most appearances and goals by a Japanese player in [[UEFA]] club competitions.<ref>{{cite news |date=16 December 2016 |title=Exportschlager: Japans Fußball-Stars in Europa |url=https://de.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/024e-0e987e088002-29802bf3ea51-1000--exportschlager-japans-fussball-stars-in-europa/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161220175414/http://de.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid=2431794.html |archive-date=20 December 2016 |access-date=30 December 2016 |work=[[UEFA]] |language=de}}</ref>

Since making his senior international debut in May 2008, he has won over 90 caps and scored over 30 goals. On 29 November 2012, Kagawa was named the [[Asian Football Confederation]]'s International Player of the Year.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/sports/football/top-stories/Shinji-Kagawa-wins-inaugural-AFC-Player-of-the-Year-award/articleshow/17418779.cms |title=Shinji Kagawa was named AFC International Player of the Year Award |publisher=indiatimes.com |date=29 November 2012 |access-date=30 November 2012 }}</ref>

==Club career==
Kagawa was born in [[Kobe]], Hyōgo Prefecture.<ref name=Hugman>{{Hugman|23623|access-date=23 January 2020}}</ref> He started playing football at the age of five. He played for Marino Football Club from 1994 to 1999,<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.kagawashinji.com/english/profile/|title=Shinji Kagawa Profile|publisher=KagawaShinji.com|language=ja|access-date=5 June 2012|archive-url=https://archive.today/20130103152110/http://www.kagawashinji.com/english/profile/|archive-date=3 January 2013|url-status=dead}}</ref> Kobe NK Football Club from 1999 to 2001, and FC Miyagi Barcelona in [[Sendai]] from 2001 to 2005.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.j-leaguers.net/special/history/file023.html|title=Shinji Kagawa – Player's History|publisher=J-League Pro-Footballers Association|language=ja|access-date=13 May 2010|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100626073905/http://www.j-leaguers.net/special/history/file023.html|archive-date=26 June 2010}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.kagawashinji.com/posts/view/28376/?aid=239 |title=Profile |publisher=Kagawa Shinji Official website |access-date=8 June 2014 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://archive.today/20140608012945/http://www.kagawashinji.com/posts/view/28376/?aid=239 |archive-date=8 June 2014 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=卒団生 プロサッカー選手 |url=http://www.mildsports.co.jp/publics/index/9/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140714145620/http://www.mildsports.co.jp/publics/index/9/ |archive-date=14 July 2014 |access-date=8 June 2014 |publisher=FC Miyagi Barcelona}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2014/jun/01/world-cup-2014-japan-profile-shinji-kagawa |title=World Cup 2014: Japan profile – Shinji Kagawa |work=The Guardian |date=1 June 2014 |access-date=8 June 2014 |archive-date=2 June 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140602163408/http://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2014/jun/01/world-cup-2014-japan-profile-shinji-kagawa |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Cerezo Osaka===
[[Cerezo Osaka]] noticed his talent and signed him at the age of 17. He was the first player in Japan to sign a professional contract before graduating from high school, except players promoted from youth teams of the J.League clubs.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2010/11/13/japanese-whirlwind-kagawa-propelling-borussia-dortmund-in-bundesliga/ |title=Japanese Whirlwind Kagawa Propelling Borussia Dortmund in Bundesliga |first=Jack |last=Bell |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=13 November 2010 |access-date=5 November 2011 |archive-date=15 October 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111015124054/http://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2010/11/13/japanese-whirlwind-kagawa-propelling-borussia-dortmund-in-bundesliga/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In 2007, he gained a regular position but the club missed promotion to the [[J.League Division 1]]. He became the top scorer of [[2009 J.League Division 2|J.League Division 2]] and drew widespread attention in 2009.

===Borussia Dortmund===
====2010–11 season====
[[File:Shinji Kagawa.jpg|thumb|upright|Kagawa with [[Borussia Dortmund]] in 2011]]
In the summer of 2010, Kagawa transferred from Cerezo to [[Borussia Dortmund]] for €350,000. The relatively low cost of the transfer was because of a release clause in Kagawa's contract with Cerezo, which allowed him to leave the club cheaply if he had an opportunity to play in Europe.<ref>{{cite web |first=Uli |last=Hesse |title=Kagawa provides reminder of Eastern promise |url=http://soccernet.espn.go.com/columns/story?id=831418&sec=europe&root=europe&cc=3888/ |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120728170520/http://soccernet.espn.go.com/columns/story?id=831418&sec=europe&root=europe&cc=3888/ |url-status=dead |archive-date=28 July 2012 |publisher=ESPN Soccernet |date=12 October 2010 |access-date=10 November 2011 }}</ref>

On 23 August 2010, he made his debut appearance in the [[Bundesliga]], starting for his new club Dortmund. After scoring his first two competitive goals for the club in the [[UEFA Europa League]] qualifying tie against [[Qarabağ FK|Qarabağ]], he continued this form with his first goal in the Bundesliga against [[VfL Wolfsburg]], which Dortmund won 2–0.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.bangkokpost.com/news/sports/195794/ballack-injured-as-10-man-hanover-hold-bayer|title=Ballack injured as 10-man Hanover hold Bayer|publisher=[[Bangkok Post]]|date=11 September 2010|access-date=11 September 2010}}</ref> In the [[Revierderby]] against arch-rivals [[FC Schalke 04|Schalke 04]], Kagawa said before the match that he would score two goals, which he then did as Dortmund won 3–1.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.goal.com/en-us/match/46585/schalke-04-vs-borussia-dortmund/report|title=Schalke 1–3 Borussia Dortmund: Prophetic Kagawa At The Double For BVB|date=19 September 2010|access-date=7 June 2011|publisher=Goal.com|archive-date=11 February 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110211140514/http://www.goal.com/en-us/match/46585/schalke-04-vs-borussia-dortmund/report|url-status=live}}</ref>

Kagawa missed the second half of the Bundesliga season because of an injury he picked up during international duty with Japan. His strong effort in the first half of the season helped his side finish as champions of the Bundesliga, and he was named in the Bundesliga Best XI, scoring eight goals in 18 appearances.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.dw-world.de/dw/article/0,,15076759,00.html |title=Dortmund celebrate Bundesliga championship |date=14 May 2011 |access-date=20 February 2012 |website=dw-world.de |archive-date=7 January 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120107184618/http://www.dw-world.de/dw/article/0,,15076759,00.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

====2011–12 season====
Kagawa was back in time for the start of the [[2011–12 Bundesliga]] campaign and quickly found his way back into the starting line-up. Kagawa scored his first goal of the new Bundesliga season on 18 September, coming in a 2–1 loss to [[Hannover 96]], as Dortmund gave up two goals in the last three minutes.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://soccernet.espn.go.com/match?id=320991&cc=5901 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120713193313/http://soccernet.espn.go.com/match?id=320991&cc=5901 |url-status=dead |archive-date=13 July 2012 |title=Hannover 96 2–1 Borussia Dortmund |date=18 September 2011 |access-date =20 February 2012 |website=ESPN Soccernet}}</ref> On 22 October, Kagawa opened the scoring in the seventh minute as Dortmund won against [[1. FC Köln|Köln]] 5–0 at the [[Signal Iduna Park]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.goal.com/en-gb/match/60749/borussia-dortmund-vs-1-fc-k%C3%B6ln/report |title=Borussia Dortmund 5–0 Koln: Five-star champions move second in Bundesliga table |date=22 October 2011 |access-date=20 February 2012 |website=Goal.com |archive-date=20 June 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180620102807/http://www.goal.com/en-gb/match/60749/borussia-dortmund-vs-1-fc-k%C3%B6ln/report |url-status=live }}</ref> Kagawa scored again on 5 November, as Dortmund brushed aside VfL Wolfsburg 5–1 and moved within two points of league leaders [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]]. Kagawa scored a late consolation against [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]] in a 2–1 loss in the group stage of the [[2011–12 UEFA Champions League|Champions League]].

On 28 January 2012, Kagawa scored twice in Dortmund's 3–1 win against [[TSG 1899 Hoffenheim|Hoffenheim]]. Kagawa scored the only goal of the game against [[SV Werder Bremen|Werder Bremen]] on 17 March, heading in a pass from [[İlkay Gündoğan]] in the eighth minute.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://soccernet.espn.go.com/report?id=320816&cc=5901 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120714072927/http://soccernet.espn.go.com/report?id=320816&cc=5901 |url-status=dead |archive-date=14 July 2012 |title=Borussia Dortmund v Werder Bremen |publisher=ESPN Soccernet |date=17 March 2012 |access-date=13 May 2012}}</ref> Kagawa scored two goals in Dortmund's 6-1 win against Köln on 25 March, keeping Dortmund five points ahead of Bayern in the title race.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://soccernet.espn.go.com/report/_/id/320802?cc=5901 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120714170321/http://soccernet.espn.go.com/report/_/id/320802?cc=5901 |url-status=dead |archive-date=14 July 2012 |title=FC Cologne v Borussia Dortmund |website=ESPN Soccernet |date=25 March 2012 |access-date =13 May 2012}}</ref> Borussia Dortmund went on to win the league title with 81 points, setting a Bundesliga record. Kagawa also scored a goal and provided an assist for Dortmund against rivals Bayern Munich in their victory in the [[2012 DFB-Pokal final]]. He scored a total of 13 goals and recorded 12 assists in the 2011-12 season<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/fifaplus/ja/articles/otd-shinji-kagawa-helped-dortmund-win-dfb-pokal-ja |title=2012年5月12日：香川真司の活躍などでドルトムントが国内2冠達成 |publisher = |accessdate=2024-05-09}}</ref>.

===Manchester United===
[[File:Kagawa Shinji in 2012.jpg|thumb|upright|Kagawa training with [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] in 2012]]
On 5 June 2012, [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] announced that they had reached an agreement with Borussia Dortmund and Kagawa for his transfer,<ref>{{cite news |title=Reds agree Kagawa transfer |url=http://www.manutd.com/en/News-And-Features/Football-News/2012/Jun/manchester-united-agree-shinji-kagawa-transfer.aspx |work=ManUtd.com |publisher=Manchester United |date=5 June 2012 |access-date=5 June 2012 |archive-date=29 June 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120629164615/http://www.manutd.com/en/news-and-features/football-news/2012/jun/manchester-united-agree-shinji-kagawa-transfer.aspx |url-status=live }}</ref> a deal that made him the first Japanese player to join the club.<ref>{{cite news |first=Adam |last=Marshall |title=Kagawa's career so far |url=http://www.manutd.com/en/News-And-Features/Football-News/2012/Jun/shinji-kagawa-career-background-after-manchester-united-agree-deal-with-dortmund.aspx |work=ManUtd.com |publisher=Manchester United |date=5 June 2012 |access-date=5 June 2012 |archive-date=8 June 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120608033031/http://www.manutd.com/en/News-And-Features/Football-News/2012/Jun/shinji-kagawa-career-background-after-manchester-united-agree-deal-with-dortmund.aspx |url-status=live }}</ref> The fee was reported to be an initial £12 million, with a further £5 million in possible add-ons.<ref>{{cite news |title=Shinji Kagawa to join Manchester United after Dortmund agree fee |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2012/jun/05/shinji-kagawa-manchester-united-borussia-dortmund |work=The Guardian |date=5 June 2012 |access-date=19 June 2018 |archive-date=19 June 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180619163623/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2012/jun/05/shinji-kagawa-manchester-united-borussia-dortmund |url-status=live }}</ref> The transfer was completed on 22 June after Kagawa passed his medical at Manchester United and received his UK work permit.<ref>{{cite news |title=Kagawa seals Reds move |url=http://www.manutd.com/en/News-And-Features/Football-News/2012/Jun/shinji-kagawa-passes-medical-and-agrees-united-move.aspx |work=ManUtd.com |publisher=Manchester United |date=22 June 2012 |access-date=22 June 2012 |archive-date=1 July 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120701153155/http://www.manutd.com/en/News-And-Features/Football-News/2012/Jun/shinji-kagawa-passes-medical-and-agrees-united-move.aspx |url-status=live }}</ref> On 3 July, it was revealed that Kagawa would wear the number 26 shirt for United.<ref>{{cite news |title=New numbers for United trio |url=http://www.manutd.com/en/News-And-Features/Football-News/2012/Jul/new-squad-numbers-for-valencia-and-new-signings.aspx |work=ManUtd.com |publisher=Manchester United |date=3 July 2012 |access-date=3 July 2012 |archive-date=13 July 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120713085056/http://www.manutd.com/en/News-And-Features/Football-News/2012/Jul/new-squad-numbers-for-valencia-and-new-signings.aspx |url-status=live }}</ref> Kagawa spoke about his former club, Borussia Dortmund, saying: "I will carry this club, their great fans, and the whole city in my heart forever and will follow their results with great interest."<ref>{{cite news |title=Shinji Kagawa: "I will carry the club, the city and the fans in my heart forever" |url=http://www.bvb.de/?%87%ECY%1B%E7%F4%9C%5El%E2%83%9A |access-date=8 July 2012 |archive-date=25 May 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130525114900/http://www.bvb.de/?%87%ECY%1B%E7%F4%9C%5El%E2%83%9A |url-status=live }}</ref>

On 18 July 2012, Kagawa was handed a two-minute appearance to mark his Manchester United debut in [[Durban]], South Africa, against [[AmaZulu F.C.|AmaZulu]] in a pre-season friendly match.<ref>{{cite web |title=AmaZulu 0–1 Manchester United |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/18895918 |work=BBC Sport |date=18 July 2012 |access-date=25 July 2012 |archive-date=23 July 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120723005600/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/18895918 |url-status=live }}</ref> Kagawa made his first start for the Red Devils against [[Ajax Cape Town F.C.|Ajax Cape Town]] on 21 July 2012,<ref>{{cite news |title=Ajax Cape Town 1–1 Man Utd |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/18940863 |publisher=BBC |date=21 July 2012 |access-date=22 July 2012 |archive-date=21 July 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120721171435/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/18940863 |url-status=live }}</ref> and scored his first goal against [[Shanghai Shenhua F.C.|Shanghai Shenhua]] four days later.<ref>{{cite news |first=Nick |last=Coppack |title=Shanghai S'hua 0 United 1 |url=http://www.manutd.com/en/Fixtures-And-Results/Match-Reports/2012/Jul/shanghai-shenhua-v-manchester-united-tour-2012-match-report.aspx |work=ManUtd.com |publisher=Manchester United |date=25 July 2012 |access-date=25 July 2012 |archive-date=27 July 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120727021245/http://www.manutd.com/en/Fixtures-And-Results/Match-Reports/2012/Jul/shanghai-shenhua-v-manchester-united-tour-2012-match-report.aspx |url-status=live }}</ref> 

His [[2012–13 Premier League|Premier League]] debut was on 20 August 2012, playing the full 90 minutes of United's 1–0 defeat away to [[Everton F.C.|Everton]].<ref>{{cite news |first=Phil |last=McNulty |title=Everton 1–0 Man Utd |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/19240566 |work=BBC Sport |date=20 August 2012 |access-date=21 August 2012 |archive-date=16 October 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131016202245/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/19240566 |url-status=live }}</ref> He scored his first goal against [[Fulham F.C.|Fulham]] on 25 August in his home debut for the club. After [[Tom Cleverley]]'s shot was parried by Fulham goalkeeper [[Mark Schwarzer]], Kagawa scored on the rebound from close range.<ref>{{cite news |first=Alistair |last=Magowan |title=Man Utd 3–2 Fulham |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/19304316 |work=BBC Sport |date=25 August 2012 |access-date=25 August 2012 |archive-date=13 November 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121113222136/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/19304316 |url-status=live }}</ref> On 19 September, Kagawa was given his first [[UEFA Champions League|Champions League]] start as United played against [[Galatasaray S.K. (football team)|Galatasaray]]. He provided an assist for the winning goal scored by [[Michael Carrick]] in a 1–0 win.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://uk.eurosport.yahoo.com/football/champions-league/2012-2013/team-men-519106.html |title=Carrick gets United off to winning start |website=eurosport.yahoo.com |date=19 September 2012 |access-date=27 December 2012 |archive-date=2 January 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140102193925/http://uk.eurosport.yahoo.com/football/champions-league/2012-2013/team-men-519106.html |url-status=live }}</ref> On 23 October, he set up [[Javier Hernández]] for United's first goal in an eventual 3–2 Champions League victory against [[S.C. Braga|Braga]]. He twisted his knee during the match, which left him sidelined for two months.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.manutd.com/en/News-And-Features/Football-News/2012/Oct/shinji-kagawa-picks-up-injury-against-braga.aspx |title=Kagawa's knee injury |publisher=Manchester United |date=23 October 2012 |access-date=27 December 2012 |archive-date=27 November 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121127115758/http://www.manutd.com/en/News-And-Features/Football-News/2012/Oct/shinji-kagawa-picks-up-injury-against-braga.aspx |url-status=live }}</ref> Kagawa returned from injury in January 2013.

On 2 March 2013, Kagawa scored his first [[Hat-trick (association football)|hat-trick]] for United and also became the first Asian player to score a [[List of Premier League hat-tricks|hat-trick in the Premier League]],<ref>{{cite news|url=http://uk.eurosport.yahoo.com/news/football-kagawa-hat-trick-sends-manchester-united-15-165938278.html|title=Football – Kagawa hat-trick sends Manchester United 15 points clear|date=2 March 2013|access-date=2 March 2013|publisher=Yahoo! Sport|archive-date=20 February 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140220204613/http://uk.eurosport.yahoo.com/news/football-kagawa-hat-trick-sends-manchester-united-15-165938278.html|url-status=live}}</ref> in his team's 4–0 home win against [[Norwich City F.C.|Norwich City]].<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www1.skysports.com/football/live/match/261834/report|title=Premier League: Shinji Kagawa hits hat-trick as Manchester United beat Norwich 4–0|date=2 March 2013|access-date=2 March 2013|publisher=Sky Sports|archive-date=4 March 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130304040807/http://www1.skysports.com/football/live/match/261834/report|url-status=live}}</ref> On 22 April, Kagawa played a part in the title-winning match against [[Aston Villa F.C.|Aston Villa]] in which United won 3–0; he became the first Japanese player to win a Premier League title. On 12 May, Kagawa was included in the United squad to play against [[Swansea City F.C.|Swansea City]] in [[Alex Ferguson]]'s last home game before retirement, which United won 2–1. On the same day, Kagawa received his Premier League medal and was voted Man of the Match.

===Return to Dortmund===
[[File:Shinji Kagawa'14.JPG|thumb|Kagawa playing for [[Borussia Dortmund]] in 2014]]
On 31 August 2014, Kagawa officially returned to his former club, Borussia Dortmund, for an undisclosed fee thought to be around £6.5 million, signing a contract until 30 June 2018.<ref name="ShinjisReturn">{{cite web|title=Shinji Kagawa kehrt zu Borussia Dortmund zurück|trans-title=Shinji Kagawa returns to Borussia Dortmund|language=de|url=http://www.bvb.de/News/Uebersicht/Shinji-Kagawa-kehrt-zu-Borussia-Dortmund-zurueck|publisher=Borussia Dortmund|access-date=31 August 2014|date=31 August 2014|archive-date=31 August 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140831153635/http://www.bvb.de/News/Uebersicht/Shinji-Kagawa-kehrt-zu-Borussia-Dortmund-zurueck|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Dortmund end Kagawa's United misery">{{cite web|title=Dortmund end Kagawa's United misery|work=ESPN.co.uk |url=http://en.espn.co.uk/football/sport/story/339307.html|access-date=19 June 2018|date=31 August 2014|archive-date=19 June 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180619163807/http://en.espn.co.uk/football/sport/story/339307.html|url-status=live}}</ref> He was assigned the shirt number 7 which was vacated by [[Jonas Hofmann]], who had left Dortmund to join [[1. FSV Mainz 05|Mainz 05]] on loan.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.bvb.de/eng/News/Overview/Number-7-Kagawa-Shinji!|title=Number 7: Kagawa|publisher=bvb.de|date=1 September 2014|access-date=1 September 2014|archive-date=17 October 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141017091951/http://www.bvb.de/eng/News/Overview/Number-7-Kagawa-Shinji!|url-status=live}}</ref> On his return debut, Kagawa scored a goal and set up another to help Dortmund win 3–1 against [[SC Freiburg]].<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.goal.com/en-us/news/87/germany/2014/09/13/5102582/borussia-dortmund-3-1-freiburg-kagawa-stars-on-dortmundl|title=Borussia Dortmund 3–1 Freiburg: Kagawa stars on Dortmund return|publisher=Goal.com|date=13 September 2014|access-date=14 September 2014|archive-date=18 February 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150218015543/http://www.goal.com/en-us/news/87/germany/2014/09/13/5102582/borussia-dortmund-3-1-freiburg-kagawa-stars-on-dortmundl|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 14 July 2017, Kagawa extended his contract with Dortmund until 2020.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.goal.com/en/news/borussia-dortmund-shinji-kagawa-bundesliga-contract/ubx77el2p8g7130nz3tv88nol|title=Kagawa signs Borussia Dortmund extension|date=14 July 2017|access-date=19 January 2018|publisher=Goal.com|archive-date=19 January 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180119235210/http://www.goal.com/en/news/borussia-dortmund-shinji-kagawa-bundesliga-contract/ubx77el2p8g7130nz3tv88nol|url-status=live}}</ref>

In the first half of the 2018–19 season, a combination of injury and coach [[Lucien Favre]]'s preference to play [[Marco Reus]] as a central attacking midfielder meant Kagawa played just four times for Dortmund. That led to Kagawa joining Turkish club [[Beşiktaş J.K.|Beşiktaş]] on loan for the remainder of the season.<ref>{{cite news |title=Shinji Kagawa joins Besiktas on loan from Borussia Dortmund |url=https://www.skysports.com/football/news/11899/11624121/shinji-kagawa-joins-besiktas-on-loan-from-borussia-dortmund |publisher=Sky Sports |date=31 January 2019 |access-date=1 February 2019 |archive-date=20 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200820143521/https://www.skysports.com/football/news/11899/11624121/shinji-kagawa-joins-besiktas-on-loan-from-borussia-dortmund |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Real Zaragoza===
On 9 August 2019, Kagawa completed a transfer to [[Real Zaragoza]] of Spain's [[Segunda División]], penning a two-year contract.<ref>{{cite news |title=Acuerdo con el Borussia Dortmund para el traspaso de Shinji Kagawa |url=https://www.realzaragoza.com/noticias/acuerdo-con-el-borussia-dortmund-para-el-traspaso-de-shinji-kagawa |publisher=Real Zaragoza |date=9 August 2019 |access-date=9 August 2019 |archive-date=10 December 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191210161217/https://www.realzaragoza.com/noticias/acuerdo-con-el-borussia-dortmund-para-el-traspaso-de-shinji-kagawa |url-status=live }}</ref> He debuted for the club on 17 August 2019, in the [[2019–20 Segunda División]] opening match against [[CD Tenerife|Tenerife]] at [[La Romareda]], which Zaragoza won 2–0.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://eldesmarque.com/zaragoza/real-zaragoza/noticias/35310-kagawa-brilla-en-su-debut|title=Kagawa brilla en su debut|trans-title=Kagawa shines in his debut|date=17 August 2019|access-date=25 August 2019|publisher=ElDesmarque|language=es|archive-date=6 February 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210206011553/https://eldesmarque.com/zaragoza/real-zaragoza/noticias/35310-kagawa-brilla-en-su-debut|url-status=live}}</ref> Eight days later, he scored his first goal in a 1–1 draw against [[SD Ponferradina|Ponferradina]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://eldesmarque.com/zaragoza/real-zaragoza/video-resumenes-zgz/35526-un-punto-agridulce-asi-fue-el-empate-del-real-zaragoza|title=Un punto agridulce: así fue el empate del Real Zaragoza|trans-title=One bittersweet point: This is how Real Zaragoza's draw went|date=25 August 2019|access-date=25 August 2019|publisher=ElDesmarque|language=es|archive-date=2 February 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210202044406/https://eldesmarque.com/zaragoza/real-zaragoza/video-resumenes-zgz/35526-un-punto-agridulce-asi-fue-el-empate-del-real-zaragoza|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 2 October 2020, Kagawa's contract was terminated by Zaragoza.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.realzaragoza.com/noticias/fin-de-la-relaci-n-contractual-con-shinji-kagawa|title=Fin de la relación contractual con Shinji Kagawa|trans-title=End of the contractual relationship with Shinji Kagawa|publisher=Real Zaragoza|language=es|date=2 October 2020|access-date=2 October 2020|archive-date=5 February 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210205072950/https://www.realzaragoza.com/noticias/fin-de-la-relaci-n-contractual-con-shinji-kagawa|url-status=live}}</ref>

===PAOK===
On 27 January 2021, Kagawa joined [[Super League Greece]] club [[PAOK FC|PAOK]] on a one-and-a-half-year contract.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.goal.com/jp/%E3%83%8B%E3%83%A5%E3%83%BC%E3%82%B9/shinji-kagawa-transfer-paok-20210124/yql95e4cyf9710k61qd75zqfv|access-date=27 January 2021|title=無所属が続いた香川真司、新天地はギリシャ1部の強豪PAOKに！1年半契約、背番号は「23」に|archive-date=7 February 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210207043850/https://www.goal.com/jp/%E3%83%8B%E3%83%A5%E3%83%BC%E3%82%B9/shinji-kagawa-transfer-paok-20210124/yql95e4cyf9710k61qd75zqfv|url-status=live}}</ref> PAOK won the Greek Cup that season, with Kagawa playing in three matches and giving one assist in the competition. On 18 December 2021, due to not being part of [[Răzvan Lucescu]]'s plans, despite some opportunities at the start of the [[2021–22 Super League Greece|2021–22 season]], Kagawa mutually terminated his contract with the club. He received no recompense for the remaining six months of his contract. Kagawa left PAOK after almost a year, having only 12 appearances in competitive games, of which only three were as a starter.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.sport24.gr/football/paok-apochorise-o-kagkava.9466309.html|access-date=18 December 2021|title=ΠΑΟΚ: Αποχώρησε ο Καγκάβα|date=18 December 2021 |archive-date=18 December 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211218181854/https://www.sport24.gr/football/paok-apochorise-o-kagkava.9466309.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Sint-Truiden===
On 10 January 2022, Kagawa signed with Belgian club [[Sint-Truidense V.V.|Sint-Truiden]].<ref>{{cite press release|publisher=[[Sint-Truidense V.V.|Sint-Truiden]]|url=https://www.stvv.com/en/news/first-team/shinji-kagawa-is-een-kanarie|title=SHINJI KAGAWA IS A CANARY!|date=10 January 2022|access-date=9 February 2022|archive-date=10 January 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220110102107/https://www.stvv.com/en/news/first-team/shinji-kagawa-is-een-kanarie|url-status=live}}</ref>

Shinji Kagawa made a total of 18 competitive appearances for Belgian club [[Sint-Truidense VV|Sint-Truiden]] across two seasons (2021–2022 and 2022–2023)<ref>{{Cite web |last=Strack-Zimmermann |first=Benjamin |title=Shinji Kagawa (Player) |url=https://www.national-football-teams.com/player/26186/Shinji_Kagawa.html |access-date=2026-06-13 |website=www.national-football-teams.com |language=en}}</ref>. A breakdown of his time with the club:{{Relevance inline|date=August 2026|reason=This could either be removed or converted to prose}} 

2021-2022 Season: six appearances in the Belgian Jupiler Pro League.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Strack-Zimmermann |first=Benjamin |title=Shinji Kagawa (Player) |url=https://www.national-football-teams.com/player/26186/Shinji_Kagawa.html |access-date=2026-06-13 |website=www.national-football-teams.com |language=en}}</ref>

2022-2023 Season: 12 appearances across all competitions, where he scored two goals.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Mobile livescore - m.Flashscore.com.ng football scores |url=https://www.flashscore.com.ng/ |access-date=2026-06-13 |website=www.flashscore.com.ng |language=en-NG}}</ref>

===Return to Cerezo Osaka===
On 31 January 2023, Kagawa returned to Japan, joining his boyhood club Cerezo Osaka on a [[Free transfer (association football)|free transfer]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.cerezo.jp/news/2023-0201-1100/|title=香川 真司選手 完全移籍加入について|trans-title=About Shinji Kagawa complete transfer joining|website=www.cerezo.jp|language=Japanese|date=31 January 2023|access-date=1 February 2023}}</ref> On 12 March, on his first start for the club in 14 years, he scored his first goal in a 2–1 win against [[Sagan Tosu]], securing their first win of the season.<ref>{{Cite web |date=12 March 2023 |title=Shinji Kagawa goal helps Cerezo secure first league win of season |url=https://www.japantimes.co.jp/sports/2023/03/12/soccer/j-league/cerezo-sagan-kagawa-goal/ |access-date=15 March 2023 |website=The Japan Times |language=en-US}}</ref>

Under head coach Arthur Papas, he made a notable impact in the opening match of the 2025 J.League season—the Osaka Derby—by scoring one goal and providing one assist in a 5–2 victory.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.soccerdigestweb.com/news/detail/id=168479 |title=J開幕戦の大阪ダービーで"理想的なボランチ"を演じた香川真司「こういう選手がいるとチームは楽」 |publisher = |accessdate=2025-02-14}}</ref>

==International career==
Kagawa played as a member of the [[Japan national under-20 football team|Japan under-20 national team]] in the [[2007 FIFA U-20 World Cup]] held in Canada.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://shinjikagawa.info/html/Video_clips_of_Shinji_Kagawa/2011/1027/Shinji_Kagawa_FIFA_U_20_World_Cup_Canada_2007_266266.html|title=Shinji Kagawa U-20 2007 World Cup Canada 2007_Shinji Kagawa|publisher=ShinjiKagawa.info|access-date=5 November 2011|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120425142526/http://shinjikagawa.info/html/Video_clips_of_Shinji_Kagawa/2011/1027/Shinji_Kagawa_FIFA_U_20_World_Cup_Canada_2007_266266.html|archive-date=25 April 2012}}</ref>

In 2008, he was selected to play as a member of the [[Japan national under-23 football team|Japan under-23 team]] for the [[2008 Summer Olympics]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.thebesteleven.com/2008/08/beijing-olympic-players-in-home-nation.html|title=Beijing Olympic Players in Home Nation Domestic Leagues|website=The Best Eleven|date=11 August 2008|access-date=5 November 2011|archive-date=4 April 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120404143039/http://www.thebesteleven.com/2008/08/beijing-olympic-players-in-home-nation.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 24 May 2008, he made his senior international debut in a friendly [[Kirin Cup]] match. [[Japan national football team|Japan]] won the match against [[Ivory Coast national football team|Ivory Coast]] 1–0. However, he was not selected in [[Takeshi Okada]]'s final 23-man squad for the [[2010 FIFA World Cup]] in South Africa. In September 2010, he scored the only goal of the game in Japan's win over [[Paraguay national football team|Paraguay]] in [[Yokohama]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.goal.com/en/news/14/asia/2010/09/04/2103105/japan-1-0-paraguay-shinji-kagawa-strike-gives-blue-samurai |title=Japan 1–0 Paraguay: Shinji Kagawa Strike Gives Blue Samurai Measure Of Revenge |website=Goal.com |date=4 September 2010 |access-date=20 February 2012 |first=Chris |last=Paraskevas |archive-date=7 September 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100907165224/http://www.goal.com/en/news/14/asia/2010/09/04/2103105/japan-1-0-paraguay-shinji-kagawa-strike-gives-blue-samurai |url-status=live }}</ref>

Kagawa was included in the [[2011 AFC Asian Cup]] by coach [[Alberto Zaccheroni]].<ref>{{cite news|title=Shinji Kagawa (Japan) 2011 AFC Asian Cup: Players to Watch|work=Fox Sports|access-date=4 October 2013|url=http://www.foxsports.com.au/galleries/gallery-fn5fbpok-1225974947405?page=1#thumb-14|archive-date=15 October 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121015181222/http://www.foxsports.com.au/galleries/gallery-fn5fbpok-1225974947405?page=1#thumb-14|url-status=live}}</ref> In the quarter-final against [[Qatar national football team|Qatar]], he scored two goals. The game ended in 3–2 win for Japan.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.goal.com/en/match/55800/japan-vs-qatar/report|title=Japan 3–2 Qatar: Kagawa Double Helps Knock Out Spirited Hosts|website=Goal.com|date=21 January 2011|access-date=6 November 2011|archive-date=18 June 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110618052357/http://www.goal.com/en/match/55800/japan-vs-qatar/report|url-status=live}}</ref> In the semi-final against [[South Korea national football team|South Korea]], Kagawa broke his metatarsal bone<ref>{{cite web |url=http://soccernet.espn.go.com/news/story/_/id/871265/injured-shinji-kagawa-could-be-out-for-the-season?cc=5739 |title=Injured Shinji Kagawa could be out for the season |website=ESPN Soccernet |date=26 January 2011 |access-date=20 February 2012 |archive-date=28 June 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110628222137/http://soccernet.espn.go.com/news/story/_/id/871265/injured-shinji-kagawa-could-be-out-for-the-season?cc=5739 |url-status=dead }}</ref> and missed the [[2011 AFC Asian Cup Final|final]] where Japan beat [[Australia men's national soccer team|Australia]] after extra-time.

Kagawa was named in Japan's 23-man squad for the [[2013 FIFA Confederations Cup]] and scored the second goal for his country during the group stage match against [[Italy national football team|Italy]], which ended in a 4–3 loss. He was selected for his first World Cup in [[2014 FIFA World Cup|2014]], but made little impact as Japan was eliminated in the group stage.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.thenationalnews.com/sport/shinji-kagawa-out-to-impress-new-manchester-united-manager-1.334064/|title=Shinji Kagawa out to impress new Manchester United manager|date=17 July 2014|access-date=20 January 2015|work=The National|archive-date=20 January 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150120154344/http://www.thenational.ae/sport/football/shinji-kagawa-out-to-impress-new-manchester-united-manager|url-status=live}}</ref>

At the [[2015 AFC Asian Cup]], Kagawa scored Japan's second goal during a 2–0 victory against [[Jordan national football team|Jordan]] in the group stage and was named as man of the match.<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://www.foxsports.com.au/football/asian-cup/up-for-the-cup-honda-and-kagawa-shine-for-japan-iraq-too-good-for-palestine/story-e6frf4fu-1227191237616|title=Up for the Cup: Honda and Kagawa shine for Japan, Iraq too good for Palestine|date=20 January 2015|work=Fox Sports}}</ref>

Kagawa participated in Japan's quarter-final match against the [[United Arab Emirates national football team|United Arab Emirates]]. After the match ended 1–1 after extra time, Kagawa, who was Japan's final kicker in the penalty shoot-out, hit the post with his kick, as Japan ended up losing the shoot-out 5–4.

Kagawa was included in Japan's World Cup squad ahead of the [[2018 FIFA World Cup]] in Russia. On 19 June, he scored a penalty against [[Colombia national football team|Colombia]] in an eventual 2-1 victory for Japan.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Murray |first=Scott |date=2018-06-19 |title=Colombia 1-2 Japan: World Cup 2018 – as it happened |url=http://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2018/jun/19/colombia-v-japan-world-cup-2018-live-updates |access-date=2026-06-16 |work=the Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref>

==Style of play==
Kagawa is a versatile attacking midfielder who can play through the middle or on either flank. He is known for his passing, movement, and positioning during attack.{{citation needed|date=August 2026}} 

While playing for Japan, he has featured in left midfield and central attacking roles. From 2023 onward, Kagawa has primarily been deployed as a central midfielder.{{citation needed|date=March 2026}}

